## Header bar

Add a header bar across the top of the page, above the tab rail, with two jobs.

**Identity.** The guide's title on the left, so it reads as a document rather
than a loose page. Keep it short.

**Section navigation.** The three sections — Orientation, Setup, Implementation —
as the top-level control. Selecting a section shows its tabs in the rail below.
The current section is clearly marked; the others are visibly available.

Two levels of navigation, in other words: sections in the header, tabs in the
rail. Someone should be able to tell at a glance both where they are and how much
of the course remains.

Constraints:

- Self-contained. No CDN, no external fonts or scripts — this runs offline from
  local disk.
- Consistent with the existing visual language of the file. Don't introduce a
  parallel style system.
- Readable at projector distance. This gets screen-shared in sessions.
- The header should not dominate. It's orientation, not the content.

If a detail here is ambiguous, ask rather than guessing.

