## Content briefs

Enough to build placeholders with real structure. **Ask before inventing
specifics** — especially anything about my company's systems, policies, or team.
Placeholders should have headings and shape, not fabricated content.

### Welcome to Agentic Coding

The hook. What these tools actually do, shown rather than described. Sets
expectations for the series: this is not autocomplete, and it rewards people who
can *specify* work clearly. Also carries the roadmap and the showcase date, since
this is the tab people land on first.

### LLM Basics

Enough mental model to use the tools well and not be surprised by them. Tokens,
context windows, why the model has no memory between sessions, why it's
confidently wrong sometimes. Worked through a chat interface everyone already has
access to — no install required to follow along.

### Agentic Coding as a Team

How we coordinate so we don't build five versions of the same thing.

The mechanism: a **shared spreadsheet** for intake — idea, what it does, who
submitted it. Deliberately a spreadsheet and not a Git repo, because during
collection the barrier to contributing has to be near zero. A repo is the
eventual destination, not the on-ramp.

Then **voting**, each person getting a limited budget of three to five votes. The
limit is the point — unlimited votes means everything ranks equally. Vote totals
establish priority. Difficulty gets assessed after, then ideas get owners.

Also: before ranking, check whether a thing already exists, internally or
commercially. Cheapest possible filter.

### Architecture and Data Flow

The tab in progress. Content unchanged — only its placement.

### Installing CLIs

Getting the CLIs onto a machine. Environments vary — a Windows workstation versus
Linux HPC clusters — and the clusters have no outbound internet, so artifacts get
downloaded on the workstation and copied over.

### Configuring CLIs

Deliberately separate from installation, because configuration is what actually
breaks. Auth against the internal gateway, config files, proxy environment
variables, and verifying the setup works before moving on.

### Context Files and Guardrails

`AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` — what they are, how they're structured, how to write
instructions that land.

Paired with this, and the reason it comes first in Implementation: **rules for
working with real data.** Which files are safe to let an agent read, what never
goes in a prompt, what stays on the cluster. I have the actual guidelines — this
tab quotes them rather than paraphrasing. Ask me for the language.

One distinction the tab must make explicitly: a line in `AGENTS.md` is an
*instruction to a model*, not enforcement. Real boundaries come from sandbox and
approval settings in the config file. Easy thing for this audience to get wrong.

### Coding with the Agent

Day-to-day practice. Scoping a task, prompting, reviewing output, iterating.
Knowing when *not* to reach for the agent — a check with a yes-or-no answer and no
judgment involved should be a script, not a prompt.

### Working with Git

Taught on the app they've already been building, not abstractly. They'll have
uncommitted changes they care about, which is the only circumstance in which Git
makes sense to anyone.

Local only. Remotes deliberately come later — say so out loud, because people will
want to push to a remote the same day they learn `git commit`.

### Build Phase

The through-line app takes shape. Less new material, more working sessions.

### GitLab: Sharing Your Repo

Remotes, SSH keys, pushing. Once local Git is muscle memory.

### Showcase

Each person demos what they built. Set the date early and keep referencing it.

